<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Atlas Mountains</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as High Atlas, Atlas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Atlas Mountains are a mountain range in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, running through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> movement began among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masmuda">Masmuda</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> of the High Atlas, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tinmel">Tinmel</a>, from where it challenged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire</a>. The mountains also bordered the territories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains.jpg" alt="Atlas Mountains" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mounir Neddi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plateau_Yagour,_Agdal,_Morocco.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Almohad Caliphate originated not as a dynastic succession or military coup but as a religious reform movement of extraordinary ideological intensity, born in the <b>Atlas Mountains</b> of Morocco in 1121 CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>By the 1130s the Almoravids were fighting losing wars simultaneously against Christian armies in the north and the Almohad insurgency in the <b>Atlas Mountains</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hafsids took their name from Abu Hafs Umar al-Hintati, a Masmuda Berber chief from the <b>High Atlas</b> who was among the closest companions of Ibn Tumart, the founder of the Almohad movement, and a leading figure of the early Almohad Caliphate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The country split: in 1359 Hintata tribesmen from the <b>High Atlas</b> occupied Marrakesh, which they ruled until 1526; in 1374 the empire was partitioned into kingdoms of Fez and Marrakesh; and wandering Arab Bedouin spread anarchy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marinid-sultanate">Marinid Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>An <b>Atlas</b> rocket placed Project SCORE in orbit.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-18">18 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Titteri, created around 1548, covered the Tell <b>Atlas</b> and high plains south of Algiers, with Médéa as its seat.</blockquote><a href="/state/beylik-of-titteri">Beylik of Titteri</a></li></ul></section>
